 
          <p>The purpose of writing my <a href="/post/2026/04/a-commentary-on-genai-inspected-through-different-lenses/">commentary on genAI inspected through different lenses</a> was, beyond summarising much-needed critique, to make my peace with the direction society is taking with this, and to stop wasting words on something I cannot change anyway.</p>
<p>The ridiculous and honestly quite depressing news related to genAI does not stop. I have to get a few more things off my chest related to the recent events.</p>
<h2 id="did-you-use-ai-to-make-this">&ldquo;Did you use AI to make this?&rdquo;</h2>
<p>I completely rewrote our <em>Java Advanced</em> course material. The previous manual was a PDF from 2024 containing examples of an outdated version of the Spring Boot framework and did not contain enough context for students to help understand why we are learning these different concepts. Needless to say, it was a lot of work, but as a lecturer, I get paid to do this, and I like to think that I helped improve the course and thus a part of the entire curriculum.</p>
<p>The first thing students asked me was &ldquo;did you use AI to make this new course?&rdquo;. I&rsquo;ll be honest. I am still offended by this. Just to be clear: I did not. I know that unfortunately, nowadays many colleagues generate their material. They claim they validated and reviewed it and used the extra time to pass more quality time with the students but this time I would like to call out the bullshit: it&rsquo;s just an excuse to do less. This is one of your core responsibilities for God&rsquo;s sake. By rewriting the course by hand, I also force myself to learn the specifics and make the exercises to ensure everything is correct. Preparing coursework also becomes easier because I know everything by heart now.</p>
<p>I understand that nowadays we have to be wary about everything and anything: is this generated? Did someone double-check these words and claims? The latest models make it more and more difficult to spot the differences between something generated and something hand-crafted. Which brings us to the next case&hellip;</p>
<h2 id="the-sad-big-walk-use-case">The Sad Big Walk Use Case</h2>
<p>Te video game <em>Big Walk</em> is being declared ineligible for the Independent Games Festival (IGF) Game Of The Year awards <a href="https://www.rockpapershotgun.com/big-walk-developer-house-house-reveal-genai-use-in-final-stretch-of-development-only-revealing-it-now-as-it-has-become-a-matter-of-public-interest">due to their usage of genAI</a>. This kind of blew up with conspiracies, forum threads, and reporters all speculating wildly. Why wasn&rsquo;t this known beforehand? Because Steam does not enforce a disclosure on usage of genAI code or chatbot usage. Many people think the IGF jurors did the right thing as they stated they do not accept <em>anything</em> that even remotely touched genAI.</p>
<p>It is becoming more and more difficult to pinpoint what exactly that means: is generating code using genAI? Clearly, vibe coding a game and submitting it to a random store frontend is: you did not make anything, you only watched something being made. Parts of generated assets or code being copied in will probably also count for the IGF.</p>
<p>But as far as I understand it, the <em>Big Walk</em> case wasn&rsquo;t that obvious: they did not use a code generation agent. They were stuck on a very technical rendering problem in Unity for which they first contacted Unity. Even support couldn&rsquo;t help them out. And only <em>then</em> they went to a chatbot and presented their problem which ultimately helped them get unstuck. This is the modern way of searching the internet for an obscure forum post where some other person bumped into the same problem and uploaded their solution to circumvent it. So yes, they used &ldquo;AI&rdquo; to search for a fix to a very technical problem, but no, they did not generate anything. Definitions&mdash;always a thin line. I think the general public&rsquo;s very exaggerated reaction to this is unfortunate. Which brings us to the next case&hellip;</p>
<h2 id="ai-usage-transparency">AI Usage Transparency</h2>
<p>Why do we demand more transparency when it comes to AI usage from small indie game studios like <em>Big Walk</em>&rsquo;s but are we totally OK with slop software on our phones, in our browsers, office tools, digital administration, and more? I&rsquo;m all for more transparency when it comes to AI usage&mdash;precisely because (1) what indeed is &ldquo;AI usage&rdquo; exactly and (2) as stated it becomes more and more difficult to see if AI has in fact been used.</p>
<p>Jeff Kaufman recently wrote about <a href="https://www.jefftk.com/p/ai-usage-transparency">AI Usage Transparency</a> on his blog where he openly admits to using Claude for generation of both words and ideas. Every article published since that post now ends with a small section detailing this usage:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Claude estimates it contributed 3% of words and 30% of ideas; it also built the new maps, processed the historical schedule and bus performance data, and made the charts, while I directed what to show and wrote the post.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I can only applaud being more transparent about it<sup id="fnref:1"><a href="#fn:1" class="footnote-ref" role="doc-noteref">1</a></sup> even though my own opinion of using genAI for blog posting is very much different. If you need Claude to generate ideas on what to write on, why exactly are you writing in the first place? Before someone emails me to ask &ldquo;did you use AI to make this post?&rdquo; I&rsquo;ll answer it now: Wouter estimates he contributed 100% of words and 100% of ideas. I&rsquo;ve seen blog post output rates go up exponentially on some posts where they happily churn out two or more posts daily when before it was once a week. My reaction to that was to unsubscribe from their RSS feed. Please stop joining the slop churn. More is not better. We collectively need to <strong>slow down</strong>, not speed up.</p>
<p>Jeff&rsquo;s AI Usage Transparency post is quite clear. That is the exception to the rule. For instance, here&rsquo;s the <a href="https://www.vrt.be/vrtnws/nl/algemeen/ai-beleid/">VRT NWS AI charter</a>, our local government&rsquo;s public service broadcaster. They start with the bold statement that usage of genAI is almost inevitable and will become more and more prevalent in the future:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Artificiële intelligentie zal een steeds grotere rol spelen in alle domeinen van onze samenleving, ook in de media in het algemeen en de journalistiek in het bijzonder. (Artificial intelligence will play an increasingly significant role in all areas of our society, including in the media in general and journalism in particular.)</p>
</blockquote>
<p>To start the charter with an excuse to use genAI everywhere when possible seems like a good way kick things into gear. The article then continues: when do they use AI? For news gathering, translations and summaries, data driven sports reporting, tailored news, image and video editing techniques, illustrations and background themes, standard audio editing techniques, anonymous testimonials. I don&rsquo;t know about you, but all I can read is &ldquo;everywhere, mwahahaha&rdquo;. As you might expect, there is no AI transparency disclaimer to be found at the end of an article.</p>
<p>I can&rsquo;t truly blame the colleague that generates course material or pushes students towards genAI instead of stimulating to ask questions to a human: our learning institution has been bickering for months about the good, the bad, and the ugly of AI. As a result, we have zero AI policies in place: none for lecturers and none for students. A neighbouring university <a href="https://www.uhasselt.be/nl/aparte-sites-uhasselt/ai-uhasselt">does have one in place</a> that, perhaps unsurprisingly, aims to &ldquo;embrace AI as the driver for innovation and societal progress&rdquo; yet they &ldquo;consider it our responsibility to teach students and staff how to engage with AI tools in a critical, ethical, and responsible manner, and to contribute to the development of trustworthy AI systems&rdquo;. I would love to see a learning institution openly say &ldquo;fuck no&rdquo; to this, but alas, I haven&rsquo;t encountered a single one. Guess what: (tainted) money flowing to these institutions also come from somewhere. Panic and fear of missing out is probably another reason.</p>
<p>At least VRT has a clearly visible AI charter put up on their site. Most companies do the same without feeling the need to publish something like that. Yet I think it&rsquo;s sad to see that you are never entirely sure that the words you are consuming are, in fact, being written by a concerned journalist. As a like-minded colleague once told me: &ldquo;if you don&rsquo;t think it&rsquo;s worth writing, then I don&rsquo;t think it&rsquo;s worth reading&rdquo;. That means we can stop consuming anything now, as the genAI usage addiction has penetrated almost every sector and spread to almost every company. Which brings us to our next case&hellip;</p>
<h2 id="you-dont-need-to-get-better-at-this">You Don&rsquo;t Need To Get Better At This</h2>
<p>I asked an ex-colleague whether their company now finally has a workable AI policy: when I left, there was a lot of chaos regarding the usage of the then still somewhat shaky technology. Their response was that they didn&rsquo;t write a single line of code in the last six months: it was all Claude doing the heavy lifting. They are working in a for them new programming language environment, churning out user story after story, but not really getting to know the language itself.</p>
<p>They pointed out to their manager that with the usage of genAI they did not have a chance to master the language themselves, literally stating that &ldquo;if you take away my Claude tools, all I can write is a nice Hello World app&rdquo;&mdash;and this person is a talented software engineer.</p>
<p>Management did not care. Yes, this person is smart enough to steer Claude in an architecturally sound way, but in a way, this evolution is effectively denying them the ability to get better at programming in another language. This is more than just sad. &ldquo;But we don&rsquo;t need to know how to program anymore&rdquo; say the futurists. I&rsquo;ll quote Dietrich Bonhoeffer&rsquo;s take on stupidity here, which is a greater threat to civilisation than evil according to him:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Stupidity is outsourcing our critical thinking to a third party, willful ignorance, and conformity.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Bonhoeffer was of course referring to blindly following the harsh political Nazi regime he had to deal with. These three concepts can also be found in Hannah Arendt&rsquo;s idea of &ldquo;the banality of evil&rdquo;: Eichmann was simply doing his job by sending Jews to a certain death. He stopped thinking for himself and conformed to the regime.</p>
<p>But what if we take Bonhoeffer&rsquo;s theory of stupidity out of its Nazi context and into the genAI discussion? The moment I ask an agent or a chatbot to do something, I turn off my brain and wait for an answer. Isn&rsquo;t that outsourcing our critical thinking to a third party? A highly centralised knowedge-locking subscription-based American third party? We know we should validate and review but the output is too big and we don&rsquo;t care. Isn&rsquo;t that willful ignorance? Everybody is increasing their output by generating, I can&rsquo;t be left behind. Isn&rsquo;t that conformity?</p>
<h2 id="in-closing">In Closing</h2>
<p>My sourdough bread baking book never reached its mainstream audience and that&rsquo;s okay. But what&rsquo;s not okay is that it is now drowning in the steady stream of fully generated slop. A few examples: <a href="https://www.bol.com/be/nl/p/het-alledaagse-zuurdesem-weggooien-kookboek/9300000291484059/?cid=1793736252398-8485362795698">Het Alledaagse Zuurdesem weggooien Kookboek</a> is fully AI-generated. The first few preview pages are horrendous. Yet it is for sale and highly rated. Another example: <a href="https://www.bol.com/be/nl/p/alles-over-zuurdesem/9300000267702792/?cid=1793736182566-8561672078630">Alles over Zuurdesem</a>. None of these books have true authors: the publisher that churns out utter crap is listed as author. Yet if people unaware of the small text &ldquo;AI-generated&rdquo; (or unaware of its meaning) are searching for &ldquo;zuurdesem&rdquo;&mdash;the Dutch word for sourdough&mdash;they now find more slop works than genuine informative books. This makes me more than sad: this makes me furious.</p>
<p>Every single day I have to deal with the AI acronym and every single day I read stories like this: <a href="https://www.timeextension.com/news/2026/11/i-saw-no-path-forward-atari-homebrew-awards-cancelled-following-flood-of-ai-made-games">Atari Homebrew Awards Cancelled Following Flood of AI-Made Games</a>. This is even happening in the retro computing community. Why would you even want to grab your AI toolkit? The whole purpose of retro computing is to have <strong>to learn and to have fun</strong> exploring and programming on old hardware. Not to choke someone with your generated shit. In case you&rsquo;re not aware, I&rsquo;m still furious.</p>
<p>I&rsquo;ll end with a link to Isaac Lyman&rsquo;s <a href="https://isaaclyman.com/blog/posts/against-ai/">incomplete case against generative AI</a> who built a huge evidence database of more depressing cases. It&rsquo;s not like this capitalist society will listen: the people that discovered a quick buck can be made by uploading generated stuff will hardly be convinced. Yet I have to try to learn to deal with it.</p>
